researchers have discovered jellyfish  sleep and have the same trouble that we  do waking up since jellyfish are very  low on the animal family tree the work  suggests that the ability to sleep  evolved quite early so how can you tell  an animal is napping and not just say  resting or in a coma for one we are  unresponsive to gentle nudging or sounds  but wake up immediately if we fall out  of bed or hear a loud enough noise if we  cut our sleep short we are groggy the  next day and sleep more soundly when we  finally do get back to bed  researchers decided to see if a  jellyfish whose nervous system isn't  centralized and doesn't include a brain  could also sleep they looked at several  species of upside-down jellyfish in the  genus Cassiopeia which tends to hang out  motionless with its modified tentacles  facing upward instead of downward like  corals but unlike most jellyfish  Cassiopeia hosts algae in their bodies  that help supply nutrients they pulse  their bowels once per second keeping  water flowing over their bodies to sweep  in food and clear away waste  the researchers monitor the pulsing of  23 animals day and night for almost a  week during the day the jellies pulsed  about 60 times per minute at night  the jellyfish slowed to about 39 pulses  to see if they were really asleep the  researchers lifted the animals off their  preferred place on the bottom of the  tanks and measured how quickly the  jellyfish started swimming back to the  bottom like a groggy person slow to get  moving in the morning the jellyfish were  slow to respond to being moved but they  did wake up if they were lifted again 30  seconds later they swim immediately down  to the bottom finally when some  jellyfish were deprived of sleep they  were not as active the next morning but  they did recover the next day  that sleeping evolved in these animals  suggests that it's not just brains that  need sleep but that individual nerve  cells may crave it as well it might also  mean that sleep was present in the very  first animals to exist on this planet  [Music]  you
 

